---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CoordinateEditor from '../components/CoordinateEditor';
import '../styles/coordinate.css';
const url = 'https://www.chartsai.com/coordinate-plane-generator/';
const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
    name: 'Coordinate Plane Generator',
    applicationCategory: 'EducationalApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Web browser',
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
    url,
    author: { '@id': 'https://www.chartsai.com/#organization' },
    featureList: [
      'Blank coordinate grids',
      'Labelled point plotting',
      'Coordinate practice with answer keys',
      'A4 and US Letter PDF',
      'SVG, PNG and CSV export',
    ],
  },
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://www.chartsai.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Printables', item: 'https://www.chartsai.com/printables/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Coordinate plane generator', item: url },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Coordinate Plane Generator — Plot Points & Print Free PDF | ChartsAI"
  description="Create a blank coordinate plane, plot labelled points or make a practice worksheet with an answer key. Free A4 and Letter PDF, SVG and PNG. No signup."
  schema={schema}
  imagePath="/coordinate/assets/quadrants.png"
  imageAlt="Five labelled points on a coordinate plane, including the origin and all four quadrants"
  imageWidth={720}
  imageHeight={720}
>
  <section class="container coordinate-intro">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/printables/">Printables</a><span>/</span><span
        >Coordinate plane generator</span>
    </nav>
    <span class="eyebrow">FREE MATH TOOL · EXPLORE & PRINT</span>
    <h1>Coordinate plane<br /><em>generator.</em></h1>
    <p>
      This free coordinate plane generator makes blank grids, labelled point diagrams and practice worksheets.
      Choose one or four quadrants, add your points, then download a graph or a printable PDF with an optional
      answer key.
    </p>
  </section>
  <div class="container editor-container">
    <CoordinateEditor client:load /><noscript
      ><p class="notice">
        Enable JavaScript to customize the grid. The ready-made PDFs and worked example below are available
        without JavaScript.
      </p></noscript>
  </div>
  <section class="container section" aria-labelledby="ready-title">
    <span class="eyebrow">NEED A SHEET RIGHT NOW?</span><h2 id="ready-title">Ready to print. Free to use.</h2>
    <div class="coordinate-activities">
      <article>
        <h3>Four-quadrant grid</h3><p>
          A blank plane from −10 to 10 on both axes, numbered in ones. Space for a name and date.
        </p><a href="/coordinate/assets/four-quadrants-a4.pdf" download>A4 PDF ↓</a> · <a
          href="/coordinate/assets/four-quadrants-letter.pdf"
          download>Letter PDF ↓</a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>First-quadrant grid</h3><p>
          Axes from 0 to 10, with positive coordinates. A clear starting point for learning ordered pairs.
        </p><a href="/coordinate/assets/first-quadrant-a4.pdf" download>A4 PDF ↓</a> · <a
          href="/coordinate/assets/first-quadrant-letter.pdf"
          download>Letter PDF ↓</a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Plot a triangle</h3><p>
          Plot A (1, 1), B (7, 1) and C (4, 6). Connect them to make a triangle. The second page gives the
          answer.
        </p><a href="/coordinate/assets/triangle-a4.pdf" download>A4 + answers ↓</a> · <a
          href="/coordinate/assets/triangle-letter.pdf"
          download>Letter + answers ↓</a>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p>
      Portrait orientation. Choose the matching paper size and print at 100% / Actual size. These grids
      preserve equal X and Y units; a unit is not a fixed millimetre length.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="container section coordinate-worked">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">A WORKED EXAMPLE</span><h2>Across first.<br /><em>Then up or down.</em></h2><p>
        An ordered pair (x, y) tells you where a point sits. Start at the origin (0, 0). Move horizontally by
        x, then vertically by y. Positive x goes right; negative x goes left. Positive y goes up; negative y
        goes down.
      </p><p>
        For A (3, 4), move 3 units right and 4 up. For B (−4, 3), move 4 left and 3 up. Changing the signs
        changes the quadrant. A point on an axis is not inside a quadrant.
      </p>
      <table>
        <caption>Original teaching example</caption><thead
          ><tr><th>Point</th><th>Coordinates</th><th>Position</th></tr></thead
        ><tbody
          ><tr><td>A</td><td>(3, 4)</td><td>Quadrant I</td></tr><tr
            ><td>B</td><td>(−4, 3)</td><td>Quadrant II</td></tr
          ><tr><td>C</td><td>(−3, −4)</td><td>Quadrant III</td></tr><tr
            ><td>D</td><td>(4, −3)</td><td>Quadrant IV</td></tr
          ><tr><td>O</td><td>(0, 0)</td><td>Origin</td></tr></tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        <a href="/coordinate/assets/quadrants.csv" download>Download the example points ↓</a> · <a
          href="#editor">Try “Explore four quadrants” in the editor ↑</a>
      </p>
    </div><img
      src="/coordinate/assets/quadrants.svg"
      width="720"
      height="720"
      loading="lazy"
      alt="A at (3, 4), B at (-4, 3), C at (-3, -4), D at (4, -3), and O at the origin, on equally scaled axes."
    />
  </section>
  <section class="container section coordinate-reference">
    <span class="eyebrow">MAKE A USEFUL LEARNING ACTIVITY</span><h2>
      One set of points. Several ways to learn.
    </h2>
    <p>
      <strong>For a blank handout:</strong> choose Blank grid, set the range and download PDF. Hide axis numbers
      if the task is to label them. No point data is needed.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>For a diagram:</strong> choose Labelled diagram and open “Edit or paste points.” Enter one point per
      line, such as <code>A,3,4</code>. Two-column X/Y data is also accepted; labels are assigned A, B, C in
      row order. CSV commas or pasted spreadsheet tabs separate the columns. An optional header must be X,Y or
      Label,X,Y.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>For practice:</strong> choose plotting to start with ordered pairs and an empty graph, or reading
      to start with labelled points and blank answers. Reveal the answer on screen after attempting the task. The
      practice PDF always keeps answers off page 1; the optional answer key is a separate page.
    </p>
    <h2>What stays consistent?</h2><p>
      The diagram, worksheet and answer key use the same validated point values. X and Y use the same distance
      per unit, even when their ranges differ. Joining points follows the supplied order and closes the shape;
      it does not sort points or fit a trend line. Repeated positions are preserved, although nearby labels
      can overlap.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use up to 12 points, unique labels of 1–8 letters or numbers, and coordinates between −50 and 50.
      Fractions such as 1/2 are accepted and converted to numbers; decimal points use a dot. Diagram labels
      show up to eight decimal places, while CSV retains the numeric values. Each axis must include zero and
      contain 2–40 intervals. Limits must align with the interval. Out-of-range points are flagged instead of
      silently removed.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a coordinate grid and point-plotting tool. It does not solve algebraic equations or construct
      geometric proofs. For paired measurements with a regression line, use the <a href="/scatter-plot-maker/"
        >scatter plot maker</a
      >; for a series of measurements, use the <a href="/line-graph-maker/">line graph maker</a>.
    </p>
    <h2>Questions before you download</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>Can I use just the first quadrant?</summary><p>
        Yes. Select “First quadrant: 0 to 10” under Grid range. If any existing points are negative, edit them
        or choose the “Plot a triangle” example, which uses positive coordinates. The tool will explain which
        points do not fit.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Does revealing answers put them on the worksheet?</summary><p>
        No. PDF practice sheets keep the question page separate from the answer key. SVG and PNG downloads
        reflect the graph currently shown; CSV always contains the points. Use the PDF worksheet when
        distributing practice to students.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Are the downloads free for classroom use?</summary><p>
        Yes. Print and reuse the original grids and activities for lessons, tutoring or personal study. No
        account, payment or attribution on your worksheet is required. The original source code is available
        under the MIT license.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What is the difference between PDF, SVG and PNG?</summary><p>
        PDF contains a high-resolution page at the selected A4 or Letter size, including the activity and
        optional answer key. SVG is an editable vector graph. PNG is a 2160 × 2160 graph image for slides or
        documents. CSV contains the labelled numeric coordinates.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Will my work be saved?</summary><p>
        Your edits stay in this browser tab and are not uploaded or saved to an account. Download your work
        before refreshing or leaving the page. Aggregate analytics events contain only fixed tool and format
        identifiers.
      </p>
    </details>
    <p class="byline">
      Designed and built by <a href="https://www.supamakers.com/">SupaMakers</a> · Reviewed September 8, 2026 ·
      <a href="/source/">Source and calculation checks</a>
    </p>
  </section>
<section class="container section"><h2>Keep learning with visual math tools.</h2><p>Go from ordered pairs to <a href="/slope-calculator/">slope and line equations</a>, or <a href="/geometry-transformation-calculator/">transform a polygon</a>. Explore <a href="/math-tools/">all five math tools and worksheets</a>.</p></section>
</Layout>
